flowchart TD
  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["<b>Could global macro shocks overwhelm trade deals<br/>and push the U.S. trade deficit higher?</b><br/><i>Brad Setser, CFR, June 2018</i>"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["<b>Trade balances are macroeconomic</b><br/>They reflect demand, fiscal policy, currencies, and external adjustment—not just trade deals"]
    P2["<b>U.S. fiscal stimulus needs foreign demand</b><br/>The deficit rises unless stronger U.S. demand is matched abroad"]
    P3["<b>Weak demand encourages export-led adjustment</b><br/>Countries under pressure often rely on weaker currencies and exports"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    D1["<b>Argentina and Turkey must adjust</b><br/>Large external deficits mean weaker domestic demand and less global demand growth"]
    D2["<b>2014–15 precedent</b><br/>EM weakness coincided with slower global trade and a larger U.S. non-oil deficit"]
    D3["<b>Italy/eurozone risk</b><br/>Financial and political stress could weaken the euro"]
    D4["<b>China is tightening</b><br/>Domestic weakness plus dollar strength could create depreciation pressure"]
    D5["<b>Japan faces fiscal tightening</b><br/>Consumption-tax hikes have historically weakened domestic demand"]
    D6["<b>2017 was unusually favorable</b><br/>Europe, China, Japan, and EM demand all supported the global environment"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["<b>Foreign adjustment weakens demand</b><br/>Lower domestic spending abroad reduces demand for imports"]
    M2["<b>Weaker foreign currencies strengthen the dollar</b><br/>Dollar strength hurts U.S. trade competitiveness"]
    M3["<b>Demand divergence widens the U.S. deficit</b><br/>Strong U.S. demand plus weak foreign demand raises net imports"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["<b>EM adjustment reduces global demand support</b><br/>Foreign economies contribute less to import growth"]
    C2["<b>Euro and Asian weakness could lift the dollar</b><br/>Currency shifts reinforce pressure on U.S. trade"]
    C3["<b>The 2017 macro constellation may be ending</b><br/>Foreign demand may no longer offset U.S. fiscal expansion"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["<b>Main claim: macro shocks could overpower trade deals</b><br/>The U.S. trade deficit could rise even if the administration<br/>secures new trade agreements"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Dialectic"]
    X["<b>Scale caveat</b><br/>Argentina and Turkey are individually too small to drive the full outcome"]
    QF["<b>Path uncertainty</b><br/>Italy and China's eventual adjustments are not predetermined"]
    R1["<b>Offsetting scenario</b><br/>Strong foreign demand could still counterbalance U.S. fiscal stimulus"]
  end

  P3 --> M1
  D1 --> M1
  D2 --> M1
  D5 --> M1
  M1 --> C1

  P3 --> M2
  D3 --> M2
  D4 --> M2
  M2 --> C2

  P1 --> M3
  P2 --> M3
  C1 --> M3
  C2 --> M3
  D6 --> C3
  C3 --> M3
  M3 --> K

  C1 -.->|qualified by| X
  C2 -.->|uncertain path| QF
  C3 -.->|could be offset by| R1

  C2 --> M3

  Q ~~~ P1 & P2 & P3
  P1 ~~~ D1 & D2
  P2 ~~~ D3 & D6
  P3 ~~~ D4 & D5

